Autolinks
=========

A URL with a trailing slash: https://example.com/.

Markers inside paths: https://example.com/my_page_name and https://example.com/~user/a--b...

Query string: https://example.com/search?q=a&copy=2.

Two URLs on a line: https://a.example/ and https://b.example/ here.

Trailing punctuation: https://example.com/page, (https://example.com/page) or "https://example.com/page"?

Balanced parentheses: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markup_(computing).

Inside formatting: *https://example.com/* and ~https://example.com/~.

Other schemes: ftp://ftp.example.com/file.txt and mailto:me@example.com.

With attributes: https://example.com/{target=_blank .external}.

Escaped: \https://example.com/ is not a link, \\https://example.com/ is.

In code: `https://example.com/a_b_c`.

In link text: [https://example.com/](https://example.com/).

In a note[*][Source: https://example.com/source.].

| Site                  | Note
| https://example.com/  | trailing slash

``
	https://example.com/

> curl https://example.com/

{lang=fr}
Voir https://example.com/ : ici.

Variables inside URLs: https://{{host}}/docs/ and https://example.com/{{page}}.

A variable as a URL: {{site}}, and {{page}} which is not a URL.

Wrapped in italics: /https://example.com/path/ and /https://example.com/path/, and struck out: --https://example.com/a--b--.

Not wrapped: a/https://example.com/ and \/https://example.com/.
